    {
      "id": "TWhmBau_EHvAgPHfgFhHL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 350.46985626220703,
      "y": 1301.2500610351562,
      "width": 605.3995361328125,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aR",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 711169717,
      "version": 1212,
      "versionNonce": 1236432859,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1749887498545,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When using the START command in DucklingScript, it's like\nappending that file into your main file. It adds in the\nenvironment, AND it adds in the code. It's literally like copy &\npasting the code.\n\nWe should add a new command called IMPORT that keeps the\nimports separated into that other file.\n\nWhen you import a file, DucklingScript will give the current\nenvironment user vars & functions (unless specified on what\nshould be imported). Variables and functions will be instead\nset using an object called \"WrappedDataType\". \n\nThis \"WrappedDataType\" will include the environment of the\noriginally imported file, as well as of course the data. \n\nIt'll act almost as some kind of link if you will, to the variable\ninside of the environment.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "6PYG5Ih0eDWk7G5ALYK8x",
      "originalText": "When using the START command in DucklingScript, it's like appending that file into your main file. It adds in the environment, AND it adds in the code. It's literally like copy & pasting the code.\n\nWe should add a new command called IMPORT that keeps the imports separated into that other file.\n\nWhen you import a file, DucklingScript will give the current environment user vars & functions (unless specified on what should be imported). Variables and functions will be instead set using an object called \"WrappedDataType\". \n\nThis \"WrappedDataType\" will include the environment of the originally imported file, as well as of course the data. \n\nIt'll act almost as some kind of link if you will, to the variable inside of the environment.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
